From: Yocto build fix
Subject: [PATCH] autoapp: handle QTouchEvent directly, not just synthesized mouse events

Qt's evdevtouch handler (confirmed via its own debug log on this
board) only ever calls QWindowSystemInterface::handleTouchEvent() for
this touchscreen -- never handleMouseEvent(). InputDevice previously
only reacted to QEvent::MouseButtonPress/Release/Move, which depend
entirely on Qt's touch-to-mouse synthesis fallback
(Qt::AA_SynthesizeMouseForUnhandledTouchEvents, qguiapplication.cpp)
actually firing for every touch. That fallback has several layered
preconditions -- the touch event must reach a window, not be accepted
by anything along the way, etc. -- that proved unreliable enough on
this EGLFS backend that touch was observed to be completely
non-responsive, even after the touch device was confirmed working at
the kernel and Qt-plugin level (evdevtouch successfully opens the
device and reads its calibration range) and after fixing an earlier,
separate window-focus problem.

eventFilter() is an application-level event filter, so it sees the
raw QTouchEvent (via QCoreApplication::notify()) before any of that
downstream accept/ignore-based synthesis logic even runs. Handle
QEvent::TouchBegin/TouchUpdate/TouchEnd directly instead of relying on
synthesis: extract the first touch point and feed it through the same
onTouchEvent() call handleTouchEvent() already uses for the mouse
path. The touch event is explicitly accept()ed afterward so Qt's
synthesis fallback does not also fire a QMouseEvent for the same
physical touch, which would otherwise be double-processed by the
existing mouse-based path.

Also adds temporary diagnostic logging (every event type eventFilter
sees, plus confirmation whenever a touch is actually dispatched to
eventHandler_) -- touch remained non-responsive even after this fix
went in, so the next debugging step is confirming from real device
logs whether events are reaching this class at all, rather than
continuing to reason from source alone.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [oe-specific: workaround for touch
delivery being unreliable via Qt's mouse-synthesis-from-touch fallback
on this board's EGLFS/KMS backend, plus temporary diagnostics]

